<main>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="brand">
      <img class="logo" src="/logo.svg" alt="" width="96" height="96">
      <div class="wordmark">oh-my-lisp</div>
    </div>
    <p class="tagline"><em>a Lisp for AI.</em></p>
    <p class="lead">
      oml is a persistent Lisp environment: definitions, data, and state are
      defined, run, saved, and rewritten in place, and survive restarts. You and
      an attached agent drive it through the same eval surface, extend it, and
      grow it over time — it stays yours, not a disposable session.
    </p>
    <pre class="term"><span class="cmd">$ bin/oml</span>
<span class="oml">oml&gt;</span> (def greeting "hi")
#'oml.user/greeting
<span class="oml">oml&gt;</span> (oml.kernel/save-forms "greeting.clj" '[(def greeting "hi")])
"greeting.clj"
<span class="oml">oml&gt;</span> hello there
<span class="dim">oml: not Lisp: nothing was evaluated. Lisp is evaluated when it starts with `(` or is marked with `/eval`</span>
<span class="oml">oml&gt;</span> /eval (+ 1 2)
3</pre>
    <p class="cta"><a href="https://github.com/sm-th/oh-my-lisp">GitHub →</a></p>
  </header>

  <section class="why">
    <div class="card">
      <h2>One eval surface</h2>
      <p>Everything that touches the image — a human at the REPL, loaded
      configuration, or an attached agent — acts the same way: by evaluating
      Lisp in one shared, mutable runtime namespace. No side channel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h2>Explicit persistence</h2>
      <p>Nothing survives a restart unless it was explicitly saved. Definitions
      and data round-trip through readable Lisp and EDN files, so what you build
      stays yours after the process exits and comes back.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h2>Always repairable</h2>
      <p>If loaded configuration fails, oml reports the failure and starts the
      plain REPL anyway. The image is never bricked by its own code — you can
      always get back in and fix it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h2>Layers, not the core</h2>
      <p>Reaching an AI model or agent, bounding what it may touch, and every
      interface on top are layers loaded onto the kernel in Lisp — not baked
      into it. Remove a layer and the kernel underneath is untouched.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="loop">
    <h2>How it grows</h2>
    <pre class="flow">you evaluate Lisp in the image
  → save-forms or save-and-evaluate writes it to a file
     → evaluate-file restores it the next time oml starts
        → what you built is still there, ready to build on</pre>
    <p class="note">A stateless assistant starts over every session. oml carries
    forward what you already built into it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-cta">
    <h2>Where it stands</h2>
    <p>Early, and now runnable. The kernel — the eval surface,
    explicit-file persistence, and recovery boot — is in place, with an ACP
    client bundled as the first layer on top, reaching an external coding agent
    over the Agent Client Protocol.</p>
